WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.597
Threat hunting depends on available, trustworthy telemetry.

00:00:02.597 --> 00:00:06.520
A hunter can only reason from evidence that exists, is accessible,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.303
and can be interpreted correctly.

00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:12.584
The best hypothesis will fail if the required data was never collected,

00:00:12.584 --> 00:00:15.080
is retained for too short a period,

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.360
or lacks the fields needed to identify users, hosts, commands, or destinations.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.927
This is why telemetry review is part of the hunt,

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:24.710
not a separate administrative task.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.634
Common data sources include endpoint process events, authentication logs, DNS logs,

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:32.557
proxy and firewall logs, EDR telemetry, cloud control-plane logs, identity-provider logs,

00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:33.984
and email security logs.

00:00:33.984 --> 00:00:38.264
Endpoint process events help answer questions about parent-child process chains, command lines,

00:00:38.264 --> 00:00:40.047
file execution, and persistence behavior.

00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:44.328
Authentication logs help answer questions about account use, source locations, device changes,

00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:46.111
and failed or successful access.

00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:50.391
DNS and proxy logs help answer questions about domains and outbound destinations.

00:00:50.391 --> 00:00:54.671
Cloud control-plane logs show administrative actions such as role changes, key creation,

00:00:54.671 --> 00:00:56.811
storage access, or unusual API calls.

00:00:56.811 --> 00:00:59.665
Data quality matters as much as data presence.

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:02.518
Missing fields can make a useful query impossible.

00:01:02.518 --> 00:01:05.371
Clock drift can make a timeline look wrong.

00:01:05.371 --> 00:01:08.581
Inconsistent hostnames can split one asset into several identities.

00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:12.148
NAT can cause many systems to share one source IP.

00:01:12.148 --> 00:01:16.072
Short log retention can hide the start of an activity chain.

00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:19.995
Noisy event volume can bury relevant events under routine software behavior.

00:01:19.995 --> 00:01:24.988
A hunt should account for these issues instead of pretending the data is perfect.

00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:26.415
Trust also has layers.

00:01:26.415 --> 00:01:28.555
An endpoint agent may be offline.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.695
A cloud log may be delayed.

00:01:30.695 --> 00:01:34.262
A firewall log may show connection metadata without application detail.

00:01:34.262 --> 00:01:39.612
An identity log may show a successful sign-in but not what the user did afterward.

00:01:39.612 --> 00:01:44.249
Hunters should understand what each source can prove and what it cannot prove.

00:01:44.249 --> 00:01:48.529
A proxy log may show that a system connected to a domain,

00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:53.879
but it may not prove which process made the request unless endpoint data is correlated.

00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:56.019
Telemetry gaps are valid hunt findings.

00:01:56.019 --> 00:02:00.299
If the hunt cannot answer a question because DNS logs are missing

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:04.579
from a network segment or cloud administrative logs are not retained long

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:05.229
enough,

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:06.299
document that gap.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:09.866
That outcome can drive logging improvements, retention changes, parsing fixes,

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:11.293
or new detection coverage.

00:02:11.293 --> 00:02:16.643
A beginner threat hunter should build a habit of asking three telemetry questions before querying:

00:02:16.643 --> 00:02:20.566
what source should show this behavior, what fields do I need,

00:02:20.566 --> 00:02:23.063
and what limitations could weaken my conclusion?

00:02:23.063 --> 00:02:26.986
That habit keeps the investigation grounded in evidence and prevents overconfidence.

00:02:26.986 --> 00:02:30.196
Telemetry review should also include access and privacy expectations.

00:02:30.196 --> 00:02:34.120
Hunters should use approved data sources, follow retention and handling rules,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.330
and avoid collecting more information than the hunt requires.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:40.897
Good hunting respects both security needs and responsible data handling.

00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:44.820
The point is to answer the defensive question with enough evidence,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.380
not to expose unrelated user or business activity.
